Subject: A contracted layout can promise what a slot holds, and the engine enforces it at the stores

What this does
  - `rt->aot_slot_claim[layout][slot]` holds one promise per slot: 0 none, 1..63 "an object of that
    layout or null/undefined", 0x80 "not reference counted". `qjs_aot_layout_claim` sets one.
  - `js_aot_content_store(rt, p, slot, v)` runs before a value is written into an existing slot of an
    object whose shape is a layout: a value that breaks the slot's promise sets the layout's broken
    bit, the one a shape change sets.
  - `js_aot_content_enter(rt, p)` runs right after an object adopts a layout shape (an add
    transition, a JSON adoption, a template fill) and checks every slot but the last. The last slot
    never carries a promise: the caller of an add writes it afterwards.

Why we need it
  A compiled body that loads a field of a contracted object can use the value without testing its
  tag or its shape only if every way a value reaches that slot is known to keep the promise. The
  bit makes the promise safe: a break turns every dependent body to its generic form. The hooks sit
  next to the existing prototype-epoch and layout-leave hooks and cost one load of the shape's
  layout id on a store.

Functions touched
  `js_aot_content_store`, `js_aot_content_enter` (new); `add_property`, `regvm_put_field_add_hit`,
  `JS_CallInternal` (put_field add), `json_obj_adopt_shape`, the five object-literal template fills,
  `JS_SetPropertyInternal2`, `JS_DefineProperty`, `JS_SetPrivateField`, `JS_AutoInitProperty`, the
  cold put_field handler and the three own-slot hit paths; `qjs_aot_layout_claim` (new).
  The audit that every property-value store is hooked or listed with its reason is
  `.local/specs/audit-slot-stores.sh` with `.local/specs/slot-store-allowlist.txt`.

No.

